\f1\fs22 \'91Patch?\'92 asked pretty young blonde in scrubs with pig-tails, as Sarah woke up. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How\'92d you\'97?\'92 Sarah checked for escape; the door out of the bedroom was behind the home-invader.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Nancy called,\'92 the home invader said, and smiled, too bright for Sarah\'92s sleep-fogged head. \'91She says you\'92re unwell?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah flinched, then recovered when her limbic system somehow registered, 
\f2\i friend
\f1\i0 . The friend lay adjacent on the bed, propped on an elbow, and dangling a transparent envelope containing a bandage swatch half the size of the palm of Sarah\'92s hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91For your nausea.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It seems every doctor in the Ghetto is a drug dealer,\'92 Sarah said with a croak in her voice.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m not from the Ghetto,\'92 the friend said, neatly avoiding the second implication. \'91Seriously, it\'92s just for the nausea. You\'92ll be right as Airborne rain in no time, pretty woman.\'92 She kissed Sarah\'92s cheek. \'91Now \'85\'92 The friend rose on her knees, tore open the pack, and deftly applied the sticky patch high on Sarah\'92s arm.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah applied her attention to a similar patch on the friend\'92s arm. \'91Is that what yours is for?\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The friend laughed and batted her eyes. \'93Do I look like someone who suffers from nausea?\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No,\'92 Sarah conceded. \'91Though, neither am I normally.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, I suppose the odd blimp in health must squiggle across all our charts, from time to time.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91
\f2\i Blip
\f1\i0 . I think you mean, 
\f2\i blip
\f1\i0 , not 
\f2\i blimp
\f1\i0 .\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Do I?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, in any case, no blimps in my health. Ever,\'92 Sarah affirmed and was quickly self-conscious and anxious.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No?\'92 The friend raised eyebrows above the constant smile. \'91Hmm. Well, I suppose all will be revealed when we get the results back.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s heart skipped a beat, which her brain, unfamiliar with such interruptions to service, interpreted as imminent death. She gasped. \'91Results?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sure. I sent the samples back to the lab a few minutes ago.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Samples?\'92 asked Sarah, chilled. \'91What samples?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, just the usual: blood, DNA, urine \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Blood? You mean \'85 my\'97you took samples, while I was asleep?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sure, it\'92s really the most comfortable way\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91For who?\'92 Sarah was outraged. Usually, she was the one keeping everyone off balance. \'91Wait. How\'92d you get my urine?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The crazy yet somehow comforting invader reassured Sarah with an expertly placed and weighted hand upon her thigh. \'91With no small amount of skill and experience, I assure you.\'92 The invader blushed, made her smile somehow humbler, and took a deep breath. \'91Anyway, we\'92ll have all the news in forty minutes or so.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What? Who\'97who are you?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m sorry. I\'92m Dr Logan, MD PhD.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Minimally assured she finally had her doctor, Sarah yielded a thin-lipped smile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Katherine\'97Kathy,\'92 the doctor added, and gave Sarah a warm and vigorous hug. \'91Now, I really need some shut-eye.\'92 Kathy curled up, facing away from Sarah and fell asleep.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah nudged her shoulder a couple of times; Kathy\'92s only movement was the steady and deep expansion and deflation of her lungs. Sarah\'92s summation: Kathy could have been in a coma.\
