\f1\fs22 Sarah woke to the bleating of a lamb.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy turned the alarm off, sat up, adjusted pillows and rearranged her scrubs, then took her tablet of the bedside table and lay it on her her lap. It was big enough to carry a three-course breakfast on its screen. She went still, as if rebooted, and a new thought took her attention. Leaning over the side of the bed, she retrieved another patch from her medical bag on the floor, ripped the old one off her arm and applied the fresh one with casual expertise.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, I\'92ve got about forty minutes of lucidity,\'92 she said, as if to no one, \'91so let\'92s look at the results.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah, still huddled under the covers, watched as Kathy tapped, scrolled, swiped and long-pressed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Slightly elevated temperature \'85\'92 Kathy said to herself, \'91clean DNA sequences \'85 no viruses or bacteria \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah flinched; she tried not to stare.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91\'85 toxicology clear. Aw, no recreationals?\'92 Kathy smiled and Sarah smiled back, wide-eyed and blank, like a guilty child.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Hmm,\'92 Kathy said, then stiffened. \'91Quite, extraordinary. You\'92re \'85\'92 and there Kathy lapsed into such verses of medical word salad as only a combined oncologist, virologist, rheumatologist, gynaecologist, astrologer, herbalist and vacuum-cleaner salesman could conglomerate.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Stop,\'92 Sarah rasped insistently.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy regarded Sarah pulling the blankets up to her chin. She licked and pursed her lips, and  Sarah knew beyond doubt they both had things to hide.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92 Sarah asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91None of this is a surprise to you, is it.\'92 Kathy made it a statement, not a question.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah gulped as if she would swallow Kathy, the bed, and the mansion, to make the conversation go away.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I thought not,\'92 Kathy whispered. \'91Who are you?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah never felt so fragile. \'91I\'92m just a \'85 I\'92m just \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Invincible,\'92 Kathy said, matter-of-fact, barely above a whisper.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shook like a frightened doe under the protection of the bedcovers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>After a moment of intense, pitiful empathy, Kathy recovered her bedside manner, despite being on the bed rather than beside it. \'91Well, this certainly makes your condition \'85 unprecedented.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Condition? Isn\'92t this what we\'92re already talking about, my condition?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No, I mean your other condition.\'92 When Kathy saw Sarah\'92s thoughts float away like clouds, she added. \'91You know: your pregnancy.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Pregnancy?\'92 Sarah stridently spat the word.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy ground her eyebrows together. \'91I\'92m sorry; I just thought with all the Elvi calling you Holy Momma\'97I thought you already \'85 you didn\'92t know?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Pregnant?\'92 Sarah cursed. \'91No, oh no, no \'85 I was told I can never fall pregnant.\'92 Sarah felt the skein of her life untangle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy set and clamped her perfect triangular jaw. Now was no time say anything that might inflame Sarah further.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah searched for how this could be. Her ability for imagery conjured, in glowing detail, the night of the Vegas Show, and where she spent the night after \'85 and who with.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Damn you, Tony, you taxing rapist!\'92
